<worksheet xml:id="expected-value">
    <title>Mathematical Expectation (2.2)</title>
   <page>
    <p>
      One of the most important concepts in probability theory is the expected
      value of a random variable. One can think of expected value as follows: If
      we perform an experiment numerous times, the <q>average</q> result is the
      expected value (arithmetic mean).
    </p>
    <exercise workspace=".25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose we flip a fair coin and let <m>X=1</m> if a heads appears and
          <m>X=0</m> if tails. What does your intuition tell you the <q>expected</q>
          value of <m>X</m> should be if the coin is flipped repeatedly?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
   

      <definition xml:id="def-expected-value">
   
    <title>Expected Value (B:2.2-1)</title>
    <statement>
    <p>
      If <m>X</m> is a discrete random
      variable, having a probability mass function <m>f(x)</m> and space
      <m>S</m>, and <m>u(X)</m> is any function on <m>X</m>, so that 
      <m>\sum_{x \in S} u(x) f(x)</m> exists, then
      <alert>expected value or mathematical expectation</alert> of <m>u(X)</m>,
      denoted <m>E[u(X)],</m> is 
        </p>
        <p>
          <md>
            E[u(X)] = \sum_{x \in S} u(x) f(x)
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
      </definition>
 
    
<exercise workspace="1in">

      <statement>
        <p>
      Often we are just looking at <m>E[X]=</m> 
    </p>
 </statement>
 </exercise>
 <exercise workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
  Find <m>E[X]</m>, where <m>X</m> is the outcome when we roll a fair
            die.
        </p>
      </statement>
  </exercise>
  </page>
  <page>
  <exercise workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
 We play a game where a fair coin is flipped and if a heads appear we
            get <m>\$2</m> and if tails appears we pay <m>\$1</m>. Let <m>Y</m>
            be the payment made on each game. What is the expected value of
            <m>Y</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
  </exercise>
  <exercise workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
 We play a game where a fair die is rolled. If it comes up <m>1</m>
            you win <m>\$5</m>, if it comes up <m>2</m> or <m>3</m> you win
            <m>\$2</m>, otherwise you pay <m>\$3</m>. What is the expected value
            of the game?
        </p>
      </statement>
  </exercise>

  <alert>Notes:</alert>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <p>
        The expected value of a random variable is not necessarily a value that
        the random variable can take on.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
    Let <m>Y</m> be the expected payment from a game. The game is called <em>fair</em> 
    if <fillin characters="20"/>.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
    
    <theorem xml:id="thm-expected-value-properties">
      <title>Properties of Expected Value (B:2.2-1)</title>
           
      <statement>
        <p>
           If
      the mathematical expectation <m>E</m> exists, then it satisfies the
      following properties:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>E(c) = c</m> where <m>c</m> is a constant
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>X</m> is a discrete random variable and <m>c</m> is a
            constant, then <m>E[cu(X)] = cE[u(X)].</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>c_{1}</m> and <m>c_{2}</m> are constants and <m>u_{1}</m> and
            <m>u_{2}</m> are functions of <m>X</m>, then
            <md>
              E[(c_{1}u_{1}(X) + c_{2}u_{2}(X)] = c_{1}E[u_{1}(X)]+c_{2}E[u_{2}(X)].
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
 </page>
 <page>
<exercise workspace="3in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <alert>Proofs</alert>:
    </p>
  </statement>
</exercise>
   
   <exercise workspace="3IN">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>X</m> denote a random variable that takes on any of the values
      <m>-1, 0, 1</m> with pmf
      <md>
        f(-1) = .2,\,\, f(0) = .5,\,\, f(1) = .3
      </md>
      Compute <m>E[X^{2} + 3X - 5]</m> directly and using Theorem 2.2-1.
      </p>
    </statement>
   </exercise>
</page>
<page>
  
   
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A game is played where you toss a coin. If it comes up heads you get
            <m>\$1</m>, if it comes up tails you pay <m>\$1</m>. Let <m>X</m> be
            the payment of the game (amount you win or lose). 
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the space of <m>X</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
      What is the pmf of <m>X</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
       What is <m>E[X]</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
         What does <m>E[X]</m> mean? Does the value you found make
                  sense? Why?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>       
<exercise workspace="2in">
<statement>
  <p>
     Let <m>X</m> denote a random variable that takes on any of the
            values <m>0, 1, 2</m> with pmf
            <md>
             f(0)= .3,\,\, f(1) = .5, \,\, f(2) = .2
            </md>
            Compute <m>E[3X^{2} + 2X - 4]</m> directly and using Theorem 2.2-1.
  </p>
</statement>
</exercise>
</page>
<page>
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Suppose in a stack of <m>10</m> lottery tickets, <m>2</m> are
            winners. You reach in and grab two tickets at random. Let <m>X</m>
            be the number of winning tickets. Find the space of <m>X</m>, the
            pmf of <m>X</m> and <m>E[X]</m>.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
         What is the space of <m>X</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
          What is the pmf of <m>X</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
         What is <m>E[X]</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What does <m>E[X]</m> mean? Does the value you found make
                  sense? Why?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>
       

     
<exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      For these problems, make a guess for the expected value of the random variable <m>X</m>. You do not need to
      show any work.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
          A CD has <m>12</m> songs. You like <m>8</m> of them and don't like
            <m>4</m> of them. Play <m>6</m> songs chosen at random and let
            <m>X</m> be the number of songs that you like. Make a guess for <m>E[X]</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace="0.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
         A store has <m>20</m> cartons of eggs. Four of these cartons contain
            cracked eggs. You grab five cartons of eggs at random without
            checking. Let <m>X</m> be the number of cartons that contain cracked
            eggs. Make a guess for <m>E[X]</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>
        </page>
        <page>
          <exercise workspace="6in">
          <statement>
    <p>
      Suppose <m>X</m> is a R.V. with a hypergeometric distribution.
      <m>N=N_{1}+N_{2}</m> objects ( <m>N_{1}</m> alike and <m>N_{2}</m> alike)
      and suppose you choose <m>n</m> at random. Then we know that
      <m>P(X=x) = \frac{\left(\begin{array}{c}N_1\\x\end{array} \right)\left(\begin{array}{c}N_2\\n-x\end{array} \right)}{\left(\begin{array}{c}N\\n\end{array} \right)}</m>
      so:
      <md>
        E[X]=\sum_{x=0}^{N_1}x \cdot \frac{\left(\begin{array}{c}N_1\\x\end{array} \right)\left(\begin{array}{c}N_2\\n-x\end{array} \right)}{\left(\begin{array}{c}N\\n\end{array} \right)}
      </md>
      We will compute <m>E[X]</m>.[See slides on Canvas page if you don't to take notes.]
    </p>
</statement>
          </exercise>
    <p>
      Conclusion: <m>E[X]=</m>
    </p>
    </page>
</worksheet>